<p>The cash position for the month of October is more than comfortable assuming that the anticipated CRR cut materialises. Additional rupee liquidity might result if SBI brings in its GDR proceeds and RBI is on the buy side.</p>
<p>There are no significant outflows other than the zero coupon issue on the 7th and another possible government flotation later in the month. The net surplus position of over 30,000 million rupees should see call money rates ease to single digits and price gains are likely to be seen in two to three years securities.</p>
<p>However, a note of caution needs to be sounded. If inflationary considerations keep RBI from reducing the cash reserves and the onset of the busy season results in better credit demand, we could see the liquidity in the banking system drying up. Though the possibility of such an event happening in October is slim. .Source:I-Sec debt market update</p>
